## What I did

I ruled on both scoped items. Both decisions are appended to `docs/DESIGN.oracle.md` and committed on branch `worker/oracle-20260820164325` (commit `cd6134a`). I did not open a pull request; the push needs the driver.

**OD-9 rules BL-10 (adds R1004 — batch request line format).** I granted the filed default. A request line is three whitespace-separated tokens in R1001's order: value, from-unit, to-unit. Blank lines are skipped silently. Any other token count is a failed line. Vision relied on: V3 (simplicity) — the batch line is exactly the command line, so the format is one sentence. Weighed against V2: a silent skip fabricates nothing, so it is not a silent guess.

**OD-10 rules BL-11 (adds R1005 — batch output contract).** I granted the filed default. Standard output carries one line per non-skipped request, in input order. Success prints the same bare value as single-shot. Failure prints `error: <message>` in that row's place. Standard error stays empty; exit is non-zero if any line failed. Vision relied on: V2 and V1. Weighed against V1 (OD-6 put single-shot errors on stderr for V1 reasons): in batch, the stderr option causes silent row misalignment — right-looking numbers on wrong questions — which is the worse V1 failure, and an `error:` line can never parse as a number.

**Measurement is part of both decisions.** Both formats are observed by `acceptance/S5.sh`, which the future `convert-batch` plan writes: whole-line stdout comparison, empty stderr, non-zero exit, the ESC-1 precision row from OD-4, plus a second stdin document that proves the blank-line skip. Existing §13 machinery, no new mechanism.

